      <h2 class="section__title">Fair Trade Certification | Fair Wages and Community Development</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        Fair Trade certification goes beyond basic compliance to ensure workers receive fair wages, work in safe conditions, and benefit from community development funds. Fair Trade certified factories pay the Fair Trade Minimum Price or Fair Trade Premium, which workers democratically invest in community projects like education, healthcare, and infrastructure. Fair Trade appeals to brands targeting premium segments with strong ethical positioning and consumers willing to pay verified fair labor premiums.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        Fair Trade certification requirements include fair wages meeting or exceeding living wage standards, safe working conditions with proper health and safety measures, no forced or child labor, freedom of association and collective bargaining rights, environmental protection criteria, and community development premiums. The Fair Trade Premium is an additional sum paid on top of the Fair Trade Minimum Price that workers collectively decide how to invest in their communities. This democratic investment process gives workers agency and addresses community needs identified by workers themselves.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        Fair Trade certification is particularly valuable for brands targeting consumers who prioritize worker welfare and community impact. The Fair Trade label is widely recognized by consumers as a mark of ethical production. Fair Trade certified products typically command retail price premiums of 15-25%, helping brands offset the higher production costs associated with fair wage payments. For brands positioned in premium segments with strong ethical messaging, Fair Trade certification provides both ethical substance and marketing value.
      </p>

