      <h2 class="section__title">Ethical Manufacturing Costs | Is It More Expensive?</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        Yes, ethical manufacturing costs more than conventional production, but the premium is often smaller than brands expect. Ethical manufacturing typically costs 5-10% more than conventional production due to fair wage premiums, safety investments, and certification costs. However, these costs enable 15-30% retail price premiums as consumers pay more for verified ethical products. The key question is not whether ethical manufacturing costs more, but whether the premium enables profitable positioning and reduces reputational risk.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        Fair wage premiums account for 3-5% of the cost increase, as ethical manufacturers pay above minimum wage and often provide benefits like health insurance and paid leave. Safety investments including fire suppression systems, proper ventilation, ergonomic workstations, and protective equipment add 1-2% to costs. Certification costs including audit fees, documentation systems, and compliance management add 1-3%. When combined, these elements typically total 5-10% above conventional manufacturing costs.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        However, ethical certification enables significant retail price premiums. Consumer research shows that 60-70% of consumers are willing to pay more for ethically produced clothing, with actual premiums ranging from 15-30% for certified ethical products. This premium more than covers the 5-10% production cost increase, making ethical manufacturing profitable when properly positioned and marketed to conscious consumers. Additionally, ethical manufacturing reduces reputational risk from supply chain scandals, which can cost brands millions in lost sales and brand damage.
      </p>

