      <h2 class="section__title">Ethical Clothing Manufacturing FAQ</h2>
      
      <div class="faq">
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">What makes a clothing manufacturer ethical?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Ethical clothing manufacturing ensures fair wages, safe working conditions, no forced or child labor, freedom of association, and environmental responsibility. Key certifications include BSCI for social compliance, WRAP for ethical manufacturing, Fair Trade for fair wages and trade practices, and SMETA for ethical trade audits. Genuine ethical manufacturers undergo regular third-party audits and publish audit reports demonstrating compliance with international labor standards.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">Is BSCI or WRAP certification better for ethical manufacturing?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            BSCI and WRAP serve complementary purposes. BSCI focuses on social compliance across 13 performance areas including workers' rights, fair remuneration, and occupational health and safety. WRAP certifies ethical manufacturing with zero tolerance for forced labor, child labor, and discrimination. Many ethical manufacturers hold both certifications - BSCI for comprehensive social compliance and WRAP for ethical manufacturing verification. Both are recognized by major retailers in EU, UK, and US markets.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">How do I verify a manufacturer's ethical claims?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Verify ethical claims through certification databases: BSCI at amfori.org, WRAP at wrapcompliance.org, Fair Trade at fairtrade.net. Request current audit reports and check certificate validity dates. Look for specific compliance data rather than vague statements. Ask for evidence of fair wages, working hours, and safety records. Genuine ethical manufacturers provide audit reports, verification links, and welcome third-party inspections. Be skeptical of manufacturers who refuse to share certification documentation.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">What is Fair Trade certification in clothing manufacturing?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Fair Trade certification ensures workers receive fair wages, work in safe conditions, and have community development funds. Fair Trade certified factories pay the Fair Trade Minimum Price or Fair Trade Premium, which workers democratically invest in community projects. Certification requires adherence to strict labor standards, no forced or child labor, and environmental criteria. Fair Trade appeals to brands targeting premium segments with strong ethical positioning and consumers willing to pay for verified fair labor practices.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">Do ethical clothing manufacturers cost more?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Ethical manufacturing typically costs 5-10% more than conventional production due to fair wage premiums, safety investments, and certification costs. However, ethical certification enables 15-30% retail price premiums as consumers pay more for verified ethical products. The cost difference is often offset by premium positioning, brand loyalty, and reduced reputational risk. For brands targeting conscious consumers, ethical manufacturing is both a moral imperative and a profitable business strategy.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">What are the signs of an unethical clothing manufacturer?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Red flags include refusal to share audit reports or certificates, vague ethical claims without verification, prices significantly below market rates, no transparency about working conditions, excessive overtime hours, below-minimum wages, and no worker representation systems. Unethical manufacturers often claim to be "ethical" without third-party certification. Be particularly skeptical of factories in countries with weak labor laws that make unsubstantiated ethical claims.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">Can small brands work with ethical clothing manufacturers?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Yes, many ethical manufacturers work with small brands. SDF Clothing offers 300 piece minimums for ethical production. While per-unit costs are higher than conventional manufacturing, the retail premium for ethical products can offset this. Small brands can start with capsule collections using certified ethical manufacturers and scale as volume increases. The key is finding manufacturers with appropriate MOQs and building long-term relationships that benefit both parties.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">Is Bangladesh ethical for clothing manufacturing?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Bangladesh has made significant ethical improvements since 2013, with many factories now BSCI, WRAP, and Fair Trade certified. The country faces challenges with labor rights enforcement, but certified factories demonstrate strong ethical practices through regular audits. Bangladesh offers competitive ethical manufacturing costs, making it accessible for brands of all sizes. When sourcing from Bangladesh, verify individual factory certifications rather than assuming country-wide ethical standards. SDF Clothing's Bangladesh operations hold comprehensive ethical certifications.
          </p>
        </div>
